{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018SuAhRQK7yRYdhfFaSqo9y","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# AutoOpt Framework\n2\t\n3\tAutoOpt is an automated optimization harness. It uses AI agents to iteratively improve a system's performance through a structured loop of analysis, planning, and implementation.\n4\t\n5\t## How It Works\n6\t\n7\tEach iteration has two phases, each run as a separate agent session:\n8\t\n9\t1. **Generate & Plan** — Analyze current performance, review optimization history, select the most promising optimization, and produce a reviewed implementation plan.\n10\t2. **Execute Task** — Implement the optimization, measure results, document findings, and commit changes.\n11\t\n12\tThe phases communicate through files in `autoopt-results/`. Each phase reads the outputs of previous phases and writes its own outputs.\n13\t\n14\t## Directory Structure\n15\t\n16\t### Static files (`autoopt/`)\n17\t\n18\tThese files are part of the framework and do not change during a run:\n19\t\n20\t- `autoopt_context.md` — This file. Generic framework documentation.\n21\t- `context.md` — Project-specific context: setup, goals, measurement, profiling, constraints.\n22\t- `review_guideline.md` — Guidelines for the plan review sub-agent.\n23\t- `prompts/` — The three prompt files, one per phase.\n24\t\n25\t### Dynamic files (`autoopt-results/`)\n26\t\n27\tCreated and updated during runs:\n28\t\n29\t- `baseline/` — Baseline performance measurements taken before any optimizations. Created on the first run by the Generate Task agent.\n30\t- `task.md` — The current task (overwritten each iteration).\n31\t- `log.md` — Append-only log of all completed optimizations.\n32\t- `<task_name>/` — One directory per task:\n33\t  - `plan.md` — Implementation plan (reviewed and approved).\n34\t  - `report.md` — Final detailed report with results and analysis.\n35\t  - `results/` — Measurement and profiling artifacts.\n36\t\n37\t## Conventions\n38\t\n39\t### Task Naming\n40\t\n41\tFormat: `YYYY-MM-DD-HHMM-descriptive-name`\n42\t\n43\tExample: `2026-04-10-1430-parallelize-kernel-launches`\n44\t\n45\tUse the current date and time when creating the task. The descriptive name should be kebab-case and indicate the optimization approach.\n46\t\n47\t### Results Tracking\n48\t\n49\tAll results MUST be reported relative to TWO reference points:\n50\t\n51\t1. **Baseline** — The original performance before any optimizations (`autoopt-results/baseline/`).\n52\t2. **Previous task** — The performance after the most recent completed optimization.\n53\t\n54\tThis allows tracking both cumulative progress and incremental impact.\n55\t\n56\t### Log Format\n57\t\n58\t`autoopt-results/log.md` is append-only. Each task adds one section:\n59\t\n60\t```\n61\t## <task_name>\n62\t\n63\t**Idea**: <one-line description>\n64\t\n65\t**Result**: <success/failure> — <key metric change vs baseline> (vs previous: <change>)\n66\t\n67\t**Summary**: <2-3 sentences on what was done and learned>\n68\t```\n69\t\n70\t### Commit Strategy\n71\t\n72\t- Implementation changes are committed to the project's git repository.\n73\t- `autoopt-results/` is NEVER committed or staged.\n74\t- Commit messages should be descriptive. Multiple commits per task are fine.\n75\t- If an optimization does NOT improve performance:\n76\t  1. Commit the implementation anyway (preserving it in git history).\n77\t  2. Immediately `git revert` the commit(s).\n78\t  3. This keeps the branch clean while making the work recoverable.\n79\t- After the final commit for each task (whether successful or a revert), create a tag: `autoopt/<task_name>`.\n80\t\n81\t### Baseline\n82\t\n83\tThe baseline is created once, on the first run, by the Generate Task agent. It represents the system's performance before any AutoOpt optimizations. The baseline directory should contain the same measurement artifacts that each task collects, so comparisons are straightforward.\n84\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"28de78be-dcad-4e85-b9dc-f16064adac42","uuid":"568f64a0-460a-46af-9fc7-58d75158c134","timestamp":"2026-04-15T04:17:57.782Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt/autoopt_context.md","content":"# AutoOpt Framework\n\nAutoOpt is an automated optimization harness. It uses AI agents to iteratively improve a system's performance through a structured loop of analysis, planning, and implementation.\n\n## How It Works\n\nEach iteration has two phases, each run as a separate agent session:\n\n1. **Generate & Plan** — Analyze current performance, review optimization history, select the most promising optimization, and produce a reviewed implementation plan.\n2. **Execute Task** — Implement the optimization, measure results, document findings, and commit changes.\n\nThe phases communicate through files in `autoopt-results/`. Each phase reads the outputs of previous phases and writes its own outputs.\n\n## Directory Structure\n\n### Static files (`autoopt/`)\n\nThese files are part of the framework and do not change during a run:\n\n- `autoopt_context.md` — This file. Generic framework documentation.\n- `context.md` — Project-specific context: setup, goals, measurement, profiling, constraints.\n- `review_guideline.md` — Guidelines for the plan review sub-agent.\n- `prompts/` — The three prompt files, one per phase.\n\n### Dynamic files (`autoopt-results/`)\n\nCreated and updated during runs:\n\n- `baseline/` — Baseline performance measurements taken before any optimizations. Created on the first run by the Generate Task agent.\n- `task.md` — The current task (overwritten each iteration).\n- `log.md` — Append-only log of all completed optimizations.\n- `<task_name>/` — One directory per task:\n  - `plan.md` — Implementation plan (reviewed and approved).\n  - `report.md` — Final detailed report with results and analysis.\n  - `results/` — Measurement and profiling artifacts.\n\n## Conventions\n\n### Task Naming\n\nFormat: `YYYY-MM-DD-HHMM-descriptive-name`\n\nExample: `2026-04-10-1430-parallelize-kernel-launches`\n\nUse the current date and time when creating the task. The descriptive name should be kebab-case and indicate the optimization approach.\n\n### Results Tracking\n\nAll results MUST be reported relative to TWO reference points:\n\n1. **Baseline** — The original performance before any optimizations (`autoopt-results/baseline/`).\n2. **Previous task** — The performance after the most recent completed optimization.\n\nThis allows tracking both cumulative progress and incremental impact.\n\n### Log Format\n\n`autoopt-results/log.md` is append-only. Each task adds one section:\n\n```\n## <task_name>\n\n**Idea**: <one-line description>\n\n**Result**: <success/failure> — <key metric change vs baseline> (vs previous: <change>)\n\n**Summary**: <2-3 sentences on what was done and learned>\n```\n\n### Commit Strategy\n\n- Implementation changes are committed to the project's git repository.\n- `autoopt-results/` is NEVER committed or staged.\n- Commit messages should be descriptive. Multiple commits per task are fine.\n- If an optimization does NOT improve performance:\n  1. Commit the implementation anyway (preserving it in git history).\n  2. Immediately `git revert` the commit(s).\n  3. This keeps the branch clean while making the work recoverable.\n- After the final commit for each task (whether successful or a revert), create a tag: `autoopt/<task_name>`.\n\n### Baseline\n\nThe baseline is created once, on the first run, by the Generate Task agent. It represents the system's performance before any AutoOpt optimizations. The baseline directory should contain the same measurement artifacts that each task collects, so comparisons are straightforward.\n","numLines":84,"startLine":1,"totalLines":84}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Sfm4KKK48J9jSor6XbthRb","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# GPU Prover Optimization Context\n2\t\n3\t## Setup\n4\t\n5\t### Repositories\n6\t\n7\t- stark-backend: https://github.com/powdr-labs/stark-backend, branch `v2-powdr-07-04`. Starting from this branch, start a new branch called `autoopt-<YYYY-MM-DD>` for your work.\n8\t- openvm: https://github.com/powdr-labs/openvm, branch `v2-powdr-07-04`\n9\t- powdr: https://github.com/powdr-labs/powdr, branch `openvm-v2-integration-07-04`\n10\t\n11\tThe powdr repo's Cargo.toml uses local patches for openvm and stark-backend, so all three repos must be checked out side-by-side.\n12\t\n13\t### Tools\n14\t\n15\tDownload `spec.py` for analyzing metrics files:\n16\thttps://raw.githubusercontent.com/powdr-labs/powdr/refs/heads/main/openvm/metrics-viewer/spec.py\n17\t\n18\tDownload these scripts from the powdr **main** branch (they are out-of-date on the working branch):\n19\thttps://github.com/powdr-labs/powdr/tree/main/openvm-riscv/scripts\n20\t\n21\tThis includes `basic_metrics.py` for creating combined metrics files.\n22\t\n23\t## Problem Description\n24\t\n25\tWe're analyzing OpenVM v2 with autoprecompiles (APCs). Going from 0 to 300 APCs, trace cells, bus interaction messages, and constraint instances all decrease by >2x. With the CPU prover, STARK time (excl. trace) scales accordingly. With the GPU prover, it does not.\n26\t\n27\tThe goal is to make the GPU prover's \"STARK excl trace\" metric scale with the reduction in cells, bus interaction messages, and constraint instances as APCs increase (>2x improvement from APC 0 to APC 300).\n28\t\n29\tFocus ONLY on \"STARK excl trace\" — do not optimize other proving phases like trace gen or preflight execution.\n30\t\n31\tFor more context, see [this writeup](https://gist.github.com/leonardoalt/cb8fba32b08669fc63dea4d7ab0b67af).\n32\tMake sure you read it in full and explore the linked resources (e.g. the paper).\n33\t\n34\t## Measuring Results\n35\t\n36\tBefore running any benchmark, check that the GPU is free by running `nvidia-smi`. If another process is using the GPU, wait for it to finish or kill it before proceeding. Benchmarks will fail with OOM if the GPU memory is partially occupied.\n37\t\n38\tIn the powdr repo, run:\n39\t\n40\t```bash\n41\topenvm-riscv/scripts/run_pairing.sh\n42\t```\n43\t\n44\tfor APCs {0, 100, 300}. Results go to `results/pairing/`, each config producing a `metrics.json`.\n45\t\n46\tThe script compiles the guest and generates autoprecompiles once (this is slow on the first run), then tests prover changes without recompilation.\n47\t\n48\t### Analysis Tool\n49\t\n50\tUse `spec.py` (downloaded in Setup) to analyze the metrics files:\n51\t```\n52\tpython spec.py <metrics_json_path> <experiment_name>\n53\t```\n54\t\n55\tThis gives a fine-granular breakdown of proof time. An example run:\n56\t\n57\t```\n58\t$ python spec.py https://gist.githubusercontent.com/leonardoalt/f2b810eaf2d5d37491da491f496639d1/raw/551f8590bd372dc909db3d3da2235b6a8aff70d0/metrics_pairing_v2_combined.json metrics_pairing_v2_apc300\n59\t\n60\tExperiment: metrics_pairing_v2_apc300  (OpenVM 2)\n61\t\n62\t  App Proof Basic Stats\n63\t  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n64\t  Segments                  2\n65\t  AIR Instances             623\n66\t  Columns                   106,842\n67\t  Cells                     811.18M (811,182,720)\n68\t  Constraints               54,961\n69\t  Constraint Instances      505.24M (505,240,712)\n70\t  Bus Interactions          67,357\n71\t  Bus Interaction Messages  448.46M (448,457,256)\n72\t\n73\t  Proof Time\n74\t  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n75\t  Metered Execution        0.67s (669 ms)  (  8.6%)\n76\t  App Proof Time           5.90s (5896 ms)  ( 76.1%)\n77\t    STARK (excl. trace)    2.58s (2579 ms)  ( 33.3%)\n78\t      Constraints          1.63s (1628 ms)  ( 21.0%)\n79\t        LogUp GKR          0.78s (781 ms)  ( 10.1%)\n80\t        Round 0            0.66s (662 ms)  (  8.5%)\n81\t        MLE Rounds         0.18s (182 ms)  (  2.3%)\n82\t        Other              0.00s (3 ms)  (  0.0%) (residual)\n83\t      Openings             0.51s (514 ms)  (  6.6%)\n84\t        WHIR               0.19s (188 ms)  (  2.4%)\n85\t        Stacked Reduction  0.33s (325 ms)  (  4.2%)\n86\t        Other              0.00s (1 ms)  (  0.0%) (residual)\n87\t      Trace Commit         0.43s (434 ms)  (  5.6%)\n88\t      Other                0.00s (3 ms)  (  0.0%) (residual)\n89\t    Preflight Execution    1.83s (1830 ms)  ( 23.6%)\n90\t    Set Initial Memory     0.20s (203 ms)  (  2.6%)\n91\t    Trace Gen              1.28s (1282 ms)  ( 16.6%)\n92\t    Other                  0.00s (2 ms)  (  0.0%) (residual)\n93\t  Leaf Recursion           0.50s (502 ms)  (  6.5%)\n94\t  Inner Recursion          0.41s (409 ms)  (  5.3%)\n95\t  Compression              0.27s (270 ms)  (  3.5%)\n96\t  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n97\t  Total                    7.75s (7746 ms)  (100.0%)\n98\t```\n99\t\n100\tWe would expect \"STARK (excl. trace)\" to scale with a combination of cells, bus interaction messages and constraint instances, which are all going down by a factor of >2x when going from 0 to 300 APCs. This is not the case in practice on GPU though.\n101\t\n102\t## Profiling\n103\t\n104\tNVIDIA nsight and nvidia-smi are available. Use them to understand GPU performance characteristics and bottlenecks.\n105\t\n106\t## Constraints\n107\t\n108\t- Make sure you only do work in the `stark-backend` repository, and on the (newly created) `autoopt-<YYYY-MM-DD>` branch.\n109\t- Do NOT change the underlying cryptographic protocol.\n110\t- Do NOT change the verifier.\n111\t- Do NOT optimize other proving phases, aside from \"STARK (excl. trace)\"\n112\t\n113\t## Result Files\n114\t\n115\tFor each task, collect:\n116\t- `before_apc{000,100,300}.json` — metrics before the change\n117\t- `after_apc{000,100,300}.json` — metrics after the change\n118\t- `metrics_combined.json` — created using `basic_metrics.py`\n119\t- Relevant profiling files (nsight exports, etc.)\n120\t\n121\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"28de78be-dcad-4e85-b9dc-f16064adac42","uuid":"9baf26ac-5402-426f-929e-eeed64cf1225","timestamp":"2026-04-15T04:17:57.781Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt/context.md","content":"# GPU Prover Optimization Context\n\n## Setup\n\n### Repositories\n\n- stark-backend: https://github.com/powdr-labs/stark-backend, branch `v2-powdr-07-04`. Starting from this branch, start a new branch called `autoopt-<YYYY-MM-DD>` for your work.\n- openvm: https://github.com/powdr-labs/openvm, branch `v2-powdr-07-04`\n- powdr: https://github.com/powdr-labs/powdr, branch `openvm-v2-integration-07-04`\n\nThe powdr repo's Cargo.toml uses local patches for openvm and stark-backend, so all three repos must be checked out side-by-side.\n\n### Tools\n\nDownload `spec.py` for analyzing metrics files:\nhttps://raw.githubusercontent.com/powdr-labs/powdr/refs/heads/main/openvm/metrics-viewer/spec.py\n\nDownload these scripts from the powdr **main** branch (they are out-of-date on the working branch):\nhttps://github.com/powdr-labs/powdr/tree/main/openvm-riscv/scripts\n\nThis includes `basic_metrics.py` for creating combined metrics files.\n\n## Problem Description\n\nWe're analyzing OpenVM v2 with autoprecompiles (APCs). Going from 0 to 300 APCs, trace cells, bus interaction messages, and constraint instances all decrease by >2x. With the CPU prover, STARK time (excl. trace) scales accordingly. With the GPU prover, it does not.\n\nThe goal is to make the GPU prover's \"STARK excl trace\" metric scale with the reduction in cells, bus interaction messages, and constraint instances as APCs increase (>2x improvement from APC 0 to APC 300).\n\nFocus ONLY on \"STARK excl trace\" — do not optimize other proving phases like trace gen or preflight execution.\n\nFor more context, see [this writeup](https://gist.github.com/leonardoalt/cb8fba32b08669fc63dea4d7ab0b67af).\nMake sure you read it in full and explore the linked resources (e.g. the paper).\n\n## Measuring Results\n\nBefore running any benchmark, check that the GPU is free by running `nvidia-smi`. If another process is using the GPU, wait for it to finish or kill it before proceeding. Benchmarks will fail with OOM if the GPU memory is partially occupied.\n\nIn the powdr repo, run:\n\n```bash\nopenvm-riscv/scripts/run_pairing.sh\n```\n\nfor APCs {0, 100, 300}. Results go to `results/pairing/`, each config producing a `metrics.json`.\n\nThe script compiles the guest and generates autoprecompiles once (this is slow on the first run), then tests prover changes without recompilation.\n\n### Analysis Tool\n\nUse `spec.py` (downloaded in Setup) to analyze the metrics files:\n```\npython spec.py <metrics_json_path> <experiment_name>\n```\n\nThis gives a fine-granular breakdown of proof time. An example run:\n\n```\n$ python spec.py https://gist.githubusercontent.com/leonardoalt/f2b810eaf2d5d37491da491f496639d1/raw/551f8590bd372dc909db3d3da2235b6a8aff70d0/metrics_pairing_v2_combined.json metrics_pairing_v2_apc300\n\nExperiment: metrics_pairing_v2_apc300  (OpenVM 2)\n\n  App Proof Basic Stats\n  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n  Segments                  2\n  AIR Instances             623\n  Columns                   106,842\n  Cells                     811.18M (811,182,720)\n  Constraints               54,961\n  Constraint Instances      505.24M (505,240,712)\n  Bus Interactions          67,357\n  Bus Interaction Messages  448.46M (448,457,256)\n\n  Proof Time\n  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n  Metered Execution        0.67s (669 ms)  (  8.6%)\n  App Proof Time           5.90s (5896 ms)  ( 76.1%)\n    STARK (excl. trace)    2.58s (2579 ms)  ( 33.3%)\n      Constraints          1.63s (1628 ms)  ( 21.0%)\n        LogUp GKR          0.78s (781 ms)  ( 10.1%)\n        Round 0            0.66s (662 ms)  (  8.5%)\n        MLE Rounds         0.18s (182 ms)  (  2.3%)\n        Other              0.00s (3 ms)  (  0.0%) (residual)\n      Openings             0.51s (514 ms)  (  6.6%)\n        WHIR               0.19s (188 ms)  (  2.4%)\n        Stacked Reduction  0.33s (325 ms)  (  4.2%)\n        Other              0.00s (1 ms)  (  0.0%) (residual)\n      Trace Commit         0.43s (434 ms)  (  5.6%)\n      Other                0.00s (3 ms)  (  0.0%) (residual)\n    Preflight Execution    1.83s (1830 ms)  ( 23.6%)\n    Set Initial Memory     0.20s (203 ms)  (  2.6%)\n    Trace Gen              1.28s (1282 ms)  ( 16.6%)\n    Other                  0.00s (2 ms)  (  0.0%) (residual)\n  Leaf Recursion           0.50s (502 ms)  (  6.5%)\n  Inner Recursion          0.41s (409 ms)  (  5.3%)\n  Compression              0.27s (270 ms)  (  3.5%)\n  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n  Total                    7.75s (7746 ms)  (100.0%)\n```\n\nWe would expect \"STARK (excl. trace)\" to scale with a combination of cells, bus interaction messages and constraint instances, which are all going down by a factor of >2x when going from 0 to 300 APCs. This is not the case in practice on GPU though.\n\n## Profiling\n\nNVIDIA nsight and nvidia-smi are available. Use them to understand GPU performance characteristics and bottlenecks.\n\n## Constraints\n\n- Make sure you only do work in the `stark-backend` repository, and on the (newly created) `autoopt-<YYYY-MM-DD>` branch.\n- Do NOT change the underlying cryptographic protocol.\n- Do NOT change the verifier.\n- Do NOT optimize other proving phases, aside from \"STARK (excl. trace)\"\n\n## Result Files\n\nFor each task, collect:\n- `before_apc{000,100,300}.json` — metrics before the change\n- `after_apc{000,100,300}.json` — metrics after the change\n- `metrics_combined.json` — created using `basic_metrics.py`\n- Relevant profiling files (nsight exports, etc.)\n\n","numLines":121,"startLine":1,"totalLines":121}}}
