{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01ERZYBxhj1ZCNXBq5tmD1Yr","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Plan: Pre-warm GPU memory pool at device initialization\n2\t\n3\t## Goal\n4\t\n5\tEliminate the cold-start allocation overhead in the first segment's `rs_code_matrix` by pre-warming the GPU memory pool (VPMM or cudaMallocAsync) during `GpuDevice::new()`. Per-segment metrics show `rs_code_matrix_time_ms` = 57ms for seg 0 vs 0ms for seg 1 at APC 300. The 57ms is wall time of the `rs_code_matrix()` function, which launches only asynchronous GPU kernels — the time comes from the first-time allocation of the 192MB codeword buffer through the memory pool. Segments 1+ get 0ms because the pool already has a suitable freed block. Pre-warming creates and frees a large buffer at device initialization, ensuring the pool has mapped pages ready for the first prove() call.\n6\t\n7\t## Current Code Path\n8\t\n9\t### Memory allocation flow (`crates/cuda-common/src/memory_manager/mod.rs:70-100`)\n10\t\n11\t```\n12\tDeviceBuffer::<T>::with_capacity(len)  [d_buffer.rs:94]\n13\t  → MEMORY_MANAGER.lock().allocate(len * sizeof(T))  [d_buffer.rs:97]\n14\t    → if size < pool.page_size:\n15\t        cudaMallocAsync(ptr, size, cudaStreamPerThread)  [mod.rs:77]\n16\t      else:\n17\t        pool.allocate(size)  [vm_pool.rs — VPMM: cuMemCreate + cuMemMap]  [mod.rs:89]\n18\t```\n19\t\n20\tFor the 192MB codeword buffer (`codeword_height × width × 4` bytes):\n21\t- If VPMM active: `pool.allocate()` maps new physical pages via CUDA driver API — ~50ms for 96 pages\n22\t- If VPMM fallback: `cudaMallocAsync` triggers real `cudaMalloc` to grow the pool — ~10-50ms\n23\t\n24\t### Where the 57ms occurs (`crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_pcs.rs:182-279`)\n25\t\n26\t```\n27\trs_code_matrix()                    [stacked_pcs.rs:182, #[instrument(skip_all)]]\n28\t  let mut codewords = DeviceBuffer::<F>::with_capacity(codeword_height * width);  ← COLD ALLOC\n29\t  batch_expand_pad(...)              ← async kernel launch\n30\t  mle_interpolate_stages(...)        ← async kernel launch  \n31\t  bit_rev(...)                       ← async kernel launch\n32\t  batch_ntt(...)                     ← async kernel launches\n33\t  return DeviceMatrix::new(...)\n34\t```\n35\t\n36\tThe `#[instrument]` span measures wall time. All GPU kernel launches are async (return immediately). The 57ms is dominated by the cold `with_capacity` call. For seg 1, the pool has the freed block from seg 0 → instant allocation → 0ms.\n37\t\n38\t### GpuDevice initialization (`crates/cuda-backend/src/device.rs:28-43`)\n39\t\n40\t```rust\n41\tpub fn new(config: SystemParams) -> Self {\n42\t    ensure_device_ntt_twiddles_initialized();\n43\t    let prover_config = GpuProverConfig { ... };\n44\t    let id = get_device().unwrap() as u32;\n45\t    let sm_count = get_sm_count(id).expect(\"failed to get SM count\");\n46\t    Self { config, prover_config, id, sm_count }\n47\t}\n48\t```\n49\t\n50\tNo GPU memory pre-warming currently occurs.\n51\t\n52\t## Changes\n53\t\n54\t### Change 1: Add pool pre-warming to `GpuDevice::new()` (`device.rs:28-43`)\n55\t\n56\tAfter `ensure_device_ntt_twiddles_initialized()`, add a pre-warm allocation and immediate free:\n57\t\n58\t```rust\n59\tpub fn new(config: SystemParams) -> Self {\n60\t    ensure_device_ntt_twiddles_initialized();\n61\t    \n62\t    // Pre-warm the GPU memory pool by allocating and freeing a 256MB buffer.\n63\t    // This maps physical pages in VPMM (or grows the cudaMallocAsync pool),\n64\t    // avoiding cold-start overhead in the first segment's rs_code_matrix.\n65\t    {\n66\t        use openvm_cuda_common::d_buffer::DeviceBuffer;\n67\t        let warmup_elems = POOL_WARMUP_BYTES / std::mem::size_of::<u32>();\n68\t        let _warmup = DeviceBuffer::<u32>::with_capacity(warmup_elems);\n69\t        // _warmup dropped here → pages return to pool, ready for reuse\n70\t    }\n71\t    \n72\t    let prover_config = GpuProverConfig { ... };\n73\t    // ... rest unchanged\n74\t}\n75\t```\n76\t\n77\t### Change 2: Use fixed 256MB warmup size (`device.rs`)\n78\t\n79\tThe warmup uses a fixed 256MB allocation rather than a heuristic derived from system parameters. The actual codeword buffer size depends on runtime stacking decisions (which vary per segment), so any parameter-based estimate would be fragile. 256MB covers the codeword buffer at APC 300 (192MB) and most APC 0 configurations, while being only ~1% of a 24GB GPU.\n80\t\n81\t```rust\n82\t/// Fixed pool warmup size in bytes. Covers the RS codeword buffer for typical workloads.\n83\tconst POOL_WARMUP_BYTES: usize = 256 * 1024 * 1024;\n84\t```\n85\t\n86\tAt APC 300 seg 0: actual codeword = 192MB. Fits within 256MB warmup.\n87\tAt APC 0 seg 0: actual codeword varies. If > 256MB, partial benefit (pool has some mapped pages ready).\n88\t\n89\t**Alternative**: The VPMM already supports pre-allocation via the `VPMM_PAGES` environment variable (parsed in `VpmmConfig::from_env()`, `vm_pool.rs:42`). Setting `VPMM_PAGES=128` would achieve the same result without code changes. The code-based approach is preferred because it's automatic and doesn't require env var configuration.\n90\t\n91\t### Why this works\n92\t\n93\tThe memory pool (VPMM or cudaMallocAsync) keeps freed memory available for reuse:\n94\t- **VPMM path**: Physical pages are mapped via `cuMemCreate` + `cuMemMap` during warmup. On free, pages return to the pool's free list (still mapped). Next allocation reuses mapped pages without driver API calls.\n95\t- **cudaMallocAsync path**: The async pool grows via real `cudaMalloc` during warmup. On free, memory stays in the pool. Next allocation finds a suitable block immediately.\n96\t\n97\tIn both cases, the warmup allocation + free takes ~50ms during `GpuDevice::new()` (outside any timing span), and subsequent allocations of ≤256MB are instant.\n98\t\n99\t## Invariants\n100\t\n101\t1. **No behavioral change**: The warmup allocates and immediately frees — no lasting side effect except warmed pool state.\n102\t2. **Memory safety**: The warmup buffer is owned by a local scope and dropped deterministically.\n103\t3. **No peak memory increase**: The warmup buffer is freed before any other allocation occurs.\n104\t4. **APC 0 compatibility**: The cap of 256MB limits over-allocation. APC 0 may allocate larger codeword buffers (if stacking width > 8), in which case the warmup provides partial benefit. No regression.\n105\t5. **VPMM not available**: If VPMM initialization fails (page_size = usize::MAX), all allocations go through cudaMallocAsync. The warmup still helps by growing the cudaMallocAsync pool.\n106\t\n107\t## Measurement Plan\n108\t\n109\t### Commands\n110\t\n111\t```bash\n112\t# Build (ensure binary is up to date):\n113\tcd /home/georg/powdr && cargo build --bin powdr_openvm_riscv -r --features \"metrics,cuda\"\n114\t\n115\t# APC 300 (primary metric):\n116\tcd /home/georg/powdr && RUST_LOG=info target/release/powdr_openvm_riscv prove \\\n117\t  --artifact results/pairing/apc300.cbor --input 0 \\\n118\t  --metrics <results_dir>/after_apc300.json --recursion\n119\t\n120\t# APC 0 (regression check):\n121\tcd /home/georg/powdr && RUST_LOG=info target/release/powdr_openvm_riscv prove \\\n122\t  --artifact results/pairing/apc000.cbor --input 0 \\\n123\t  --metrics <results_dir>/after_apc000.json --recursion\n124\t\n125\t# Analyze:\n126\tpython3 /home/georg/spec.py <metrics_path> <experiment_name>\n127\t```\n128\t\n129\t### Expected behavior\n130\t\n131\t- **rs_code_matrix_time_ms (seg 0)**: drops from 57ms to ≤5ms\n132\t- **stacked_commit_time_ms (seg 0)**: drops from 163ms to ~110ms\n133\t- **STARK excl trace (APC 300)**: ≥30ms improvement (from ~1298ms to ≤1268ms)\n134\t- **APC 0**: ≥20ms improvement (seg 0 rs_code_matrix was 47ms)\n135\t- **No regression at APC 0**: ≤ +10ms noise on STARK excl trace\n136\t\n137\t### Key metrics to check\n138\t\n139\t- `rs_code_matrix_time_ms` for seg 0 (should match seg 1's ~0ms)\n140\t- `stacked_commit_time_ms` for seg 0 vs seg 1 gap (should shrink from 76ms to ~20ms)\n141\t- Total `STARK excl trace` at APC 300 and APC 0\n142\t\n143\t## Rollback Criteria\n144\t\n145\t1. `STARK excl trace` improvement at APC 300 is less than 10ms.\n146\t2. APC 0 `STARK excl trace` regresses by more than 20ms.\n147\t3. Any test failure in `cargo nextest run -p openvm-cuda-backend --test-threads=4`.\n148\t4. Any verification failure (invalid proofs).\n149\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"28de78be-dcad-4e85-b9dc-f16064adac42","uuid":"9e46eef3-2a18-43a2-823d-f1a9edf698aa","timestamp":"2026-04-15T04:18:01.024Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/2026-04-15-0800-cache-codeword-buffer-across-segments/plan.md","content":"# Plan: Pre-warm GPU memory pool at device initialization\n\n## Goal\n\nEliminate the cold-start allocation overhead in the first segment's `rs_code_matrix` by pre-warming the GPU memory pool (VPMM or cudaMallocAsync) during `GpuDevice::new()`. Per-segment metrics show `rs_code_matrix_time_ms` = 57ms for seg 0 vs 0ms for seg 1 at APC 300. The 57ms is wall time of the `rs_code_matrix()` function, which launches only asynchronous GPU kernels — the time comes from the first-time allocation of the 192MB codeword buffer through the memory pool. Segments 1+ get 0ms because the pool already has a suitable freed block. Pre-warming creates and frees a large buffer at device initialization, ensuring the pool has mapped pages ready for the first prove() call.\n\n## Current Code Path\n\n### Memory allocation flow (`crates/cuda-common/src/memory_manager/mod.rs:70-100`)\n\n```\nDeviceBuffer::<T>::with_capacity(len)  [d_buffer.rs:94]\n  → MEMORY_MANAGER.lock().allocate(len * sizeof(T))  [d_buffer.rs:97]\n    → if size < pool.page_size:\n        cudaMallocAsync(ptr, size, cudaStreamPerThread)  [mod.rs:77]\n      else:\n        pool.allocate(size)  [vm_pool.rs — VPMM: cuMemCreate + cuMemMap]  [mod.rs:89]\n```\n\nFor the 192MB codeword buffer (`codeword_height × width × 4` bytes):\n- If VPMM active: `pool.allocate()` maps new physical pages via CUDA driver API — ~50ms for 96 pages\n- If VPMM fallback: `cudaMallocAsync` triggers real `cudaMalloc` to grow the pool — ~10-50ms\n\n### Where the 57ms occurs (`crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_pcs.rs:182-279`)\n\n```\nrs_code_matrix()                    [stacked_pcs.rs:182, #[instrument(skip_all)]]\n  let mut codewords = DeviceBuffer::<F>::with_capacity(codeword_height * width);  ← COLD ALLOC\n  batch_expand_pad(...)              ← async kernel launch\n  mle_interpolate_stages(...)        ← async kernel launch  \n  bit_rev(...)                       ← async kernel launch\n  batch_ntt(...)                     ← async kernel launches\n  return DeviceMatrix::new(...)\n```\n\nThe `#[instrument]` span measures wall time. All GPU kernel launches are async (return immediately). The 57ms is dominated by the cold `with_capacity` call. For seg 1, the pool has the freed block from seg 0 → instant allocation → 0ms.\n\n### GpuDevice initialization (`crates/cuda-backend/src/device.rs:28-43`)\n\n```rust\npub fn new(config: SystemParams) -> Self {\n    ensure_device_ntt_twiddles_initialized();\n    let prover_config = GpuProverConfig { ... };\n    let id = get_device().unwrap() as u32;\n    let sm_count = get_sm_count(id).expect(\"failed to get SM count\");\n    Self { config, prover_config, id, sm_count }\n}\n```\n\nNo GPU memory pre-warming currently occurs.\n\n## Changes\n\n### Change 1: Add pool pre-warming to `GpuDevice::new()` (`device.rs:28-43`)\n\nAfter `ensure_device_ntt_twiddles_initialized()`, add a pre-warm allocation and immediate free:\n\n```rust\npub fn new(config: SystemParams) -> Self {\n    ensure_device_ntt_twiddles_initialized();\n    \n    // Pre-warm the GPU memory pool by allocating and freeing a 256MB buffer.\n    // This maps physical pages in VPMM (or grows the cudaMallocAsync pool),\n    // avoiding cold-start overhead in the first segment's rs_code_matrix.\n    {\n        use openvm_cuda_common::d_buffer::DeviceBuffer;\n        let warmup_elems = POOL_WARMUP_BYTES / std::mem::size_of::<u32>();\n        let _warmup = DeviceBuffer::<u32>::with_capacity(warmup_elems);\n        // _warmup dropped here → pages return to pool, ready for reuse\n    }\n    \n    let prover_config = GpuProverConfig { ... };\n    // ... rest unchanged\n}\n```\n\n### Change 2: Use fixed 256MB warmup size (`device.rs`)\n\nThe warmup uses a fixed 256MB allocation rather than a heuristic derived from system parameters. The actual codeword buffer size depends on runtime stacking decisions (which vary per segment), so any parameter-based estimate would be fragile. 256MB covers the codeword buffer at APC 300 (192MB) and most APC 0 configurations, while being only ~1% of a 24GB GPU.\n\n```rust\n/// Fixed pool warmup size in bytes. Covers the RS codeword buffer for typical workloads.\nconst POOL_WARMUP_BYTES: usize = 256 * 1024 * 1024;\n```\n\nAt APC 300 seg 0: actual codeword = 192MB. Fits within 256MB warmup.\nAt APC 0 seg 0: actual codeword varies. If > 256MB, partial benefit (pool has some mapped pages ready).\n\n**Alternative**: The VPMM already supports pre-allocation via the `VPMM_PAGES` environment variable (parsed in `VpmmConfig::from_env()`, `vm_pool.rs:42`). Setting `VPMM_PAGES=128` would achieve the same result without code changes. The code-based approach is preferred because it's automatic and doesn't require env var configuration.\n\n### Why this works\n\nThe memory pool (VPMM or cudaMallocAsync) keeps freed memory available for reuse:\n- **VPMM path**: Physical pages are mapped via `cuMemCreate` + `cuMemMap` during warmup. On free, pages return to the pool's free list (still mapped). Next allocation reuses mapped pages without driver API calls.\n- **cudaMallocAsync path**: The async pool grows via real `cudaMalloc` during warmup. On free, memory stays in the pool. Next allocation finds a suitable block immediately.\n\nIn both cases, the warmup allocation + free takes ~50ms during `GpuDevice::new()` (outside any timing span), and subsequent allocations of ≤256MB are instant.\n\n## Invariants\n\n1. **No behavioral change**: The warmup allocates and immediately frees — no lasting side effect except warmed pool state.\n2. **Memory safety**: The warmup buffer is owned by a local scope and dropped deterministically.\n3. **No peak memory increase**: The warmup buffer is freed before any other allocation occurs.\n4. **APC 0 compatibility**: The cap of 256MB limits over-allocation. APC 0 may allocate larger codeword buffers (if stacking width > 8), in which case the warmup provides partial benefit. No regression.\n5. **VPMM not available**: If VPMM initialization fails (page_size = usize::MAX), all allocations go through cudaMallocAsync. The warmup still helps by growing the cudaMallocAsync pool.\n\n## Measurement Plan\n\n### Commands\n\n```bash\n# Build (ensure binary is up to date):\ncd /home/georg/powdr && cargo build --bin powdr_openvm_riscv -r --features \"metrics,cuda\"\n\n# APC 300 (primary metric):\ncd /home/georg/powdr && RUST_LOG=info target/release/powdr_openvm_riscv prove \\\n  --artifact results/pairing/apc300.cbor --input 0 \\\n  --metrics <results_dir>/after_apc300.json --recursion\n\n# APC 0 (regression check):\ncd /home/georg/powdr && RUST_LOG=info target/release/powdr_openvm_riscv prove \\\n  --artifact results/pairing/apc000.cbor --input 0 \\\n  --metrics <results_dir>/after_apc000.json --recursion\n\n# Analyze:\npython3 /home/georg/spec.py <metrics_path> <experiment_name>\n```\n\n### Expected behavior\n\n- **rs_code_matrix_time_ms (seg 0)**: drops from 57ms to ≤5ms\n- **stacked_commit_time_ms (seg 0)**: drops from 163ms to ~110ms\n- **STARK excl trace (APC 300)**: ≥30ms improvement (from ~1298ms to ≤1268ms)\n- **APC 0**: ≥20ms improvement (seg 0 rs_code_matrix was 47ms)\n- **No regression at APC 0**: ≤ +10ms noise on STARK excl trace\n\n### Key metrics to check\n\n- `rs_code_matrix_time_ms` for seg 0 (should match seg 1's ~0ms)\n- `stacked_commit_time_ms` for seg 0 vs seg 1 gap (should shrink from 76ms to ~20ms)\n- Total `STARK excl trace` at APC 300 and APC 0\n\n## Rollback Criteria\n\n1. `STARK excl trace` improvement at APC 300 is less than 10ms.\n2. APC 0 `STARK excl trace` regresses by more than 20ms.\n3. Any test failure in `cargo nextest run -p openvm-cuda-backend --test-threads=4`.\n4. Any verification failure (invalid proofs).\n","numLines":149,"startLine":1,"totalLines":149}}}
