{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_017xhmxQhikogxspxA2aMipY","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Before (without change): 2134ms, 2169ms → average ~2152ms\nAfter (with change): 2271ms, 2285ms → average ~2278ms\n\nThe regression is real: about +126ms at APC 0, driven by LogUp GKR going from ~1028ms to ~1199ms (+171ms).\n\nThe warmup buffer allocation/free is disrupting the VPMM pool state, similar to the \"multistream-stacked-reduction-round0\" task that also saw a ~200ms GKR regression from allocation disruption.\n\nThe plan mentioned this could happen but predicted \"No regression at APC 0\" based on the assumption that the warmup buffer would be fully freed and the pool would return to a clean state. \n\nThat assumption was wrong — the VPMM pool's internal page layout after warmup is different enough to degrade GKR performance. I need to rethink the warmup approach. I could try using the VPMM_PAGES env var instead of going through DeviceBuffer, reduce the warmup size, use cudaMallocAsync directly, or attempt more complete page freeing, though the VPMM doesn't really support that. Looking at the memory manager code, I notice that allocations smaller than page_size go directly to cudaMallocAsync, which might be relevant here.\n\nThe warmup is 256MB, well above the typical 2MB page size, so it's forced through the VPMM path where cuMemCreate and cuMemMap allocate physical pages. When freed, these pages remain mapped in the pool's free list, changing the state for future allocations. Using cudaMallocAsync directly would bypass this, but the rs_code_matrix codeword buffer at APC 300 is 192MB and needs the VPMM path anyway, so I'd need to warm up through VPMM to match that scenario. The real issue might be something else entirely — at APC 0 the AIRs are fewer but larger, and the GKR input eval uses 8 pre-allocated threads, so the VPMM's current page state might be affecting how those buffers are laid out.\n\nLooking at the performance data, the Trace Commit improves consistently across both APCs (around -41 to -44ms), but at APC 0 the GKR regresses by 177ms, completely offsetting the gain. This suggests the 256MB warmup allocation is fragmenting the virtual address space in a way that hurts GKR's memory access patterns — adding 128 pages to the free list might cause subsequent VPMM allocations to land at suboptimal virtual addresses with worse cache behavior.\n\nThe VPMM_PAGES environment variable offers a pre-allocation approach that might avoid this fragmentation issue. But I'm realizing that warming up through cudaMallocAsync won't help the VPMM path for the codeword buffer, since that allocation bypasses the async pool entirely. I need to understand how VPMM_PAGES works to see if it's a better alternative.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1404,"cache_read_input_tokens":66313,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1404},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"28de78be-dcad-4e85-b9dc-f16064adac42","uuid":"add7fe68-1770-4100-87f8-92c34a6c97f8"}
