{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01TXDSGA16Xy54ho2HMgAmvw","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"So the NTT twiddles buffer is (2^11 - 2) = 2046 elements, each 4 bytes = 8184 bytes. That's tiny, well below the VPMM page size of 2MB, so it goes through cudaMallocAsync, not VPMM. So the twiddle init doesn't affect VPMM state.\n\nMy warmup is the first VPMM allocation. It creates 128 pages at the start of the VA space, then frees them. These pages become a free region at the start of the VA space. Then when rs_code_matrix allocates, it finds this free region and reuses it.\n\n\nBut the GKR input eval at APC 0 also allocates from VPMM, and with the warmup's 256MB free region at the start, those allocations might land at different VA addresses than they would without it. I'm thinking I should try using VPMM's initial_pages parameter instead—that way the pages get allocated and mapped during pool creation at the `#[ctor::ctor]` init, before anything else runs, which gives me more control over the initial state.\n\nThe real difference is timing: initial_pages creates the free region at the very start of VA space with no other state present, while my warmup creates it after NTT twiddle initialization. Since NTT twiddles use cudaMallocAsync and not VPMM, the pool should be untouched between init and my warmup, so both approaches should produce pages at the same VA addresses. But I'm going to test the initial_pages approach anyway to see if it makes a difference. I just noticed that DeviceBuffer's Drop calls d_free which locks the MEMORY_MANAGER, and my warmup runs in GpuDevice::new() through the MemoryManager—when the warmup buffer is freed, it records a new event on the current stream for its free region.\n\nThe problem might be stream_id tracking: the warmup runs on the main thread's stream, but GKR input eval happens on multiple threads with different streams. When GKR tries to reuse the warmup's free region, it sees a different stream_id and has to check event completion. The allocation logic prefers regions matching the current stream first...\n\nChecking stream compatibility...\n\nThe warmup's free region has the main thread's stream_id, so when GKR allocates on a different thread, it falls back to checking if the event is completed—which it should be by then since we're still in GpuDevice::new(). For rs_code_matrix in the main prover path, it probably runs on the main stream or a specific prover stream, so if the stream_id matches the warmup's stream...\n\nActually, I'm overcomplicating this. Let me try a simpler approach: set initial_pages to 128 by default in VpmmConfig and remove the warmup from GpuDevice::new(). That pre-allocates pages when the pool is created, which is cleaner.\n\nBut that would affect all users of the memory manager since cuda-common is shared. I need to check if there's a way to pass config to the memory manager instead. Looking at how it's initialized with a constructor that creates a default MemoryManager, which then uses VpmmConfig::from_env()—the only configuration option seems to be environment variables.\n\nI could try calling the VPMM's pre-allocation directly in GpuDevice::new() instead of going through the MemoryManager, but that requires access to the pool behind the mutex. Or I could add a pre_warm_pool() function to MemoryManager that allocates and frees without affecting the pool state for GKR.\n\nActually, the real issue is that the warmup regression at APC 0 is much larger than the improvement at APC 300, so maybe I should just use a much smaller warmup instead. The rollback criteria mentions a 20% regression threshold for APC 0 STARK, which I'm already exceeding.\n\nLet me try a different approach: use the VPMM_PAGES environment variable path instead, which pre-allocates during pool creation, or modify the VpmmConfig default to set initial_pages to 128 and remove the DeviceBuffer warmup from GpuDevice::new().","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1165,"cache_read_input_tokens":83854,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1165},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"28de78be-dcad-4e85-b9dc-f16064adac42","uuid":"718877b3-0bf8-41ee-97d0-b42ab219b88a"}
